// kindle — a light that spreads. funideas §6's other neighbour word: not
// "every row an oscillator that entrains" (`motes`, `sync`) but "a thing one
// row has and passes on" (`infect`).
//
// Nothing here decides that a front should travel. Three independent facts
// do, and the picture is what they add up to:
//
//   a few rows are always alight       (the sources, from `row.seed`)
//   a row catches from whoever is near  (`infect`)
//   every row goes out on its own       (`relax 0`)
//
// The last two are the whole system. `spread` against `heal` is an epidemic
// threshold: below it a source glows alone in a small halo, above it the
// light crosses the cloud and keeps crossing. That number is worth turning
// by hand, which is exactly why `infect` does not own it — recovery is a
// separate fact and `relax` was already the word for it.
//
//   row.u0  lit     0 dark → 1 alight. This is what a renderer reads.
//   row.u1  source  1 for the few rows that are never out, else 0
//   row.u3  crowd   how many are in reach (a readout)

spawn
perish

// The sources: `row.seed` is a per-row uniform in [0, 1), so this lights the
// top ~1.7% of them and leaves a soft edge rather than a hard set.
row.seed | mul 60 | sub 59 | clamp 0 1 | write row.u1

// Who is close, and catch it from whoever among them has most.
near plane.drift.@self.k.reach | write row.u3
infect row.u0 plane.drift.@self.k.spread

// Everybody goes out, all the time. Against the line above, this is the
// threshold; on its own it is why a front has a back.
row.u0 | relax 0 plane.drift.@self.k.heal | write row.u0 add

// And a source holds its own light up, so there is always something to
// catch. Gated on `row.u1`, so it is the sources' line and nobody else's.
row.u0 | relax 1 plane.drift.@self.k.stoke | mul row.u1 | write row.u0 add
